      "completion_tokens": 834,
      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Here’s a concise overview of what water is, how it’s structured, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n## 1. Chemical Identity\n- **Formula:** **H₂O** – each molecule consists of two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom.\n- **Molecular weight:** ≈ 18.015 g/mol.\n- **Bond angle:** ≈ 104.5°, giving the molecule a bent (V‑shaped) geometry.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Physical States\n| State | Typical Conditions | Key Characteristics |\n|-------|--------------------|----------------------|\n| **Solid (Ice)** | ≤ 0 °C (32 °F) | Crystalline lattice, less dense than liquid water (hence ice floats). |\n| **Liquid** | 0 °C – 100 °C (32 °F – 212 °F) at 1 atm | Highly cohesive, excellent solvent, high surface tension, high specific heat. |\n| **Gas (Water Vapor/Steam)** | ≥ 100 °C (212 °F) at 1 atm | Invisible gas; when condensed, forms clouds, fog, or steam. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Unique Physical & Chemical Properties\n| Property | Why It’s Important |\n|----------|--------------------|\n| **Polarity** | The uneven charge distribution makes water a superb solvent for ionic and polar substances. |\n| **Hydrogen bonding** | Weak bonds between water molecules give rise to high boiling point, surface tension, and the anomalous density maximum at 4 °C. |\n| **High specific heat (4.18 J·g⁻¹·K⁻¹)** | Buffers temperature changes, stabilizing climates and biological systems. |\n| **High heat of vaporization (≈ 2260 J·g⁻¹)** | Enables evaporative cooling (e.g., sweating). |\n| **Cohesion & adhesion** | Drives capillary action, essential for plant water transport. |\n| **Density anomaly** | Ice is ~9 % less dense than liquid water, allowing it to float and insulate aquatic life in winter. |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Role in the Environment & Life\n- **Universal solvent:** Dissolves salts, sugars, gases, and many organic compounds, facilitating biochemical reactions.\n- **Hydrologic cycle:** Evaporation → condensation → precipitation → runoff → infiltration → groundwater → back to the oceans.\n- **Biological functions:** Constitutes ~60 % of the human body, participates in metabolism, temperature regulation, nutrient transport, and waste removal.\n- **Climate regulation:** Oceans store ~93 % of Earth’s heat; water vapor is a potent greenhouse gas.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Sources & Distribution\n- **Earth’s surface:** ~71 % covered by oceans (≈ 97 % of all water).\n- **Freshwater:** ~2.5 % of total water; most is locked in glaciers, ice caps, and groundwater.\n- **Atmosphere:** Small fraction (~0.001 % of total) but crucial for weather and climate dynamics.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Everyday Examples\n- **Drinking water:** Provides essential hydration and minerals.\n- **Cooking:** Transfers heat efficiently.\n- **Cleaning:** Dissolves and carries away dirt and oils.\n- **Industrial uses:** Coolants, solvents, steam generation, hydroelectric power.\n\n---\n\n### Quick Takeaway\nWater is a polar, hydrogen‑bonded molecule (H₂O) that exists naturally as ice, liquid, and vapor. Its unique physical properties—high heat capacity, surface tension, solvent ability, and density anomaly—make it indispensable for Earth’s climate, ecosystems, and all known forms of life."
